### Why does local state break under a load balancer?

With one instance, you could keep a session in memory. With multiple instances and a load balancer, consecutive requests from the same user may land on different instances. Instance A's session is invisible to instance B.

```java
// WRONG: in-memory session map
private final Map<String, Session> sessions = new ConcurrentHashMap<>();
// Request 1 → instance A creates session
// Request 2 → instance B has no idea → user appears logged out
```

### How do you fix it?

#### Option 1: Externalize to Redis

```java
package com.bank.session;

import org.springframework.data.redis.core.StringRedisTemplate;
import java.time.Duration;

public class RedisSessionStore {

    private final StringRedisTemplate redis;

    public RedisSessionStore(StringRedisTemplate redis) {
        this.redis = redis;
    }

    public void save(String sessionId, String json) {
        redis.opsForValue().set("session:" + sessionId, json, Duration.ofMinutes(30));
    }

    public String find(String sessionId) {
        return redis.opsForValue().get("session:" + sessionId);
    }
}
```

#### Option 2: Stateless JWT

```java
// JWT carries identity in every request; no server-side session
String token = Jwts.builder()
    .setSubject("CUST-1001")
    .claim("role", "customer")
    .signWith(secretKey)
    .compact();
// Any instance can validate and extract claims without shared state
```

### Why is sticky sessions a weak fallback?

Sticky sessions pin a user to one instance, but if that instance dies the session is lost. It also undermines balancing because hot users pile onto one instance.

### Why doesn't round robin guarantee equal work?

| Algorithm | Balances | Weakness |
|---|---|---|
| Round robin | Request count | Ignores request cost |
| Least connections | In-flight requests | Accounts for slow requests |
| Least response time | Actual speed | Routes to fastest instance |

```java
// Round robin: instance A gets a 5-second report, instance B gets a 5ms lookup
// Both got 1 request, but A is overloaded and B is idle
// Least connections would route the NEXT request to B (fewer in-flight)
```

### Why must health checks verify the instance can serve?

A naive liveness check confirms the process is running. But an instance can be up yet unable to serve: DB connection pool exhausted, downstream unreachable.

```java
package com.bank.health;

import org.springframework.boot.actuate.health.Health;
import org.springframework.boot.actuate.health.HealthIndicator;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Component;
import javax.sql.DataSource;

@Component
public class ReadinessCheck implements HealthIndicator {

    private final DataSource dataSource;

    public ReadinessCheck(DataSource dataSource) {
        this.dataSource = dataSource;
    }

    @Override
    public Health health() {
        try (var conn = dataSource.getConnection()) {
            if (conn.isValid(1)) return Health.up().build();
        } catch (Exception e) { /* pool exhausted */ }
        return Health.down().build();
    }
}
```

### What are common mistakes?

```java
// WRONG: health check only checks process is alive
@GetMapping("/health")
public String health() { return "OK"; } // says OK even with dead DB

// CORRECT: health check verifies DB and dependencies
@GetMapping("/health/ready")
public Health ready() {
    return dbPool.isValid() ? Health.up().build() : Health.down().build();
}
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

- Statelessness is the foundation of horizontal scaling; any local state is a scaling bug.
- Distinguish liveness (should orchestrator restart?) from readiness (should LB send traffic?).
- A readiness check that is too strict can cascade: if a shared dependency blips and all instances report unready, you take down the whole service.
- Match the balancing algorithm to request-cost variance.
